2026-07-12T20:41:11.7250647Z     
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7250905Z     The ``pytest`` framework makes it easy to write small tests, yet
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7251354Z     scales to support complex functional testing for applications and libraries.
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7251732Z     
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7251928Z     An example of a simple test:
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7252292Z     
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7252497Z     .. code-block:: python
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7252722Z     
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7252922Z         # content of test_sample.py
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7253177Z         def inc(x):
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7253394Z             return x + 1
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7253606Z     
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7253777Z     
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7253959Z         def test_answer():
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7254203Z             assert inc(3) == 5
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7254428Z     
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7254599Z     
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7254780Z     To execute it::
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7254969Z     
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7255145Z         $ pytest
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7255423Z         ============================= test session starts =============================
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7255770Z         collected 1 items
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7255988Z     
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7256189Z         test_sample.py F
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7256410Z     
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7256662Z         ================================== FAILURES ===================================
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7257240Z         _________________________________ test_answer _________________________________
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7257325Z     
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7257419Z             def test_answer():
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7257517Z         >       assert inc(3) == 5
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7257615Z         E       assert 4 == 5
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7257707Z         E        +  where 4 = inc(3)
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7257787Z     
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7257902Z         test_sample.py:5: AssertionError
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7258051Z         ========================== 1 failed in 0.04 seconds ===========================
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7258132Z     
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7258211Z     
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7258908Z     Thanks to ``pytest``'s detailed assertion introspection, you can simply use plain ``assert`` statements. See `getting-started <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/getting-started.html#our-first-test-run>`_ for more examples.
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7259233Z     
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7259316Z     
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7259402Z     Features
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7259498Z     --------
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7259581Z     
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7260027Z     - Detailed info on failing `assert statements <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/assert.html>`_ (no need to remember ``self.assert*`` names)
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7260109Z     
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7260205Z     - `Auto-discovery
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7260481Z       <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/goodpractices.html#python-test-discovery>`_
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7260588Z       of test modules and functions
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7260666Z     
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7260913Z     - `Modular fixtures <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/fixtures.html>`_ for
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7261084Z       managing small or parametrized long-lived test resources
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7261164Z     
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7261422Z     - Can run `unittest <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/unittest.html>`_ (or trial)
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7261527Z       test suites out of the box
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7261609Z     
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7261703Z     - Python 3.10+ or PyPy3
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7261785Z     
2026-07-12T20:41:11.7262394Z     - Rich plugin architecture, with over 1300+ `external plugins <https://docs.pytest.org/en/latest/reference/plugin_list.html>`_ and thriving community
